{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129082 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128896 :question/id #uuid "97f55dee-3f6b-5c1f-a901-b391ab37a0df" :question/math-academy-id "e-3483"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404315}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"} :step/id #uuid "40fae29f-2e89-52fe-9afd-d197bd48df89" :step/math-academy-id 11664 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129897}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129083 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128897 :question/id #uuid "cfe7b419-9067-5b8c-b9c2-d4ec91120333" :question/math-academy-id "e-3486"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404322}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"} :step/id #uuid "c5d06736-c0a2-5014-b8ec-523edb383b95" :step/math-academy-id 11666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130332 :tutorial/content "For any point on the unit circle, we can draw a right triangle whose hypotenuse is the line segment between the origin and the point.\n\n![](images/b1/b1ec08957fd0d09179853d6d56c20b7058c4531bdd7e766c5b3462ba246fce2b.png)\n\nEach hypotenuse is the radius of the unit circle and consequently has a length equal to $1.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of *any* point on the unit circle must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1.\n$$\n\nWe can use this fact to find one coordinate of a point on the unit circle given the other.\n\nFor example, suppose that the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$ in the fourth quadrant on the unit circle is $\\dfrac{3}{7}.$ To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{3}{7}$ into the above and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t          \\left(\\dfrac{3}{7}\\right)^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{9}{49}+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= 1-\\dfrac{9}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty^2&= \\dfrac{40}{49}\\[5pt]\n\t\ty&= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{49}}\\[5pt]\n\t        y &= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}\n\n}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the *fourth* quadrant, $y$ must be *negative*. So we select the negative answer. Therefore, $y=-\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "123bbfdb-b303-5c97-b319-aea0e9be6205" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2720 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ba029153-c2b2-5d85-9274-f3a6cf3e00a9" :step/math-academy-id 13319 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129894}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130333 :tutorial/content "By the Pythagorean theorem, we know that the equation \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = 1\n$$\n\nholds for any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle. \n\nNow, we also know that for any point on the unit circle, the coordinates $(x,y)$ are related to the central angle $\\theta$ via the formulas\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the Pythagorean Identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equation is called the **Pythagorean trigonometric identity**, and it holds for *every* angle $\\theta.$ This includes negative angles and angles that are larger than one full rotation." :tutorial/id #uuid "2adb34b1-9f8d-5f02-bafd-029ac9251084" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2721 :tutorial/title "The Pythagorean Identity"} :step/id #uuid "169bc7d9-7551-55a5-9cfd-107f346c1bed" :step/math-academy-id 13322 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129895}}] :activity/title "Extending the Pythagorean Identity to All Quadrants" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129363}]}
